Another interesting idea which could apply to weapons is proficiency.  A
character would always be proficient in whatever weapon(s) they started
with, however, to become proficient with a new weapon would require time.
There would be a penalty applied to your chance of hitting when using a
non-proficient weapon.

Since I'm on the subject of new ideas, how about changing the way
spellbooks work.  Rather than have the item used up when reading it,
instead, simply have the character be able to cast the spell a limited
number of times (HARD = 1, NOT HARD = 2-5, it could depend on level, luck,
etc.).  Then when the character ran out of memorized spells, they would
need to reread the book.  This has a beneficial effect of downgrading the
impact of reading a scroll of amnesia, but a disadvantage of forcing the
character to carry around that heavy spellbook all the time.  Perhaps
you could have spellbooks which contain more than one spell.

How about the idea of a new spell called bless?  This would have the
effect of changing an uncursed item to a blessed one.  It would not
uncurse an item (remove curse would be needed for that).  Perhaps it
could be a special ability of only priests/knights (like turning undead)
thereby helping add some benefits to those classes.

Another idea for a ring is a vampric ring of regeneration.  This ring
would only give back hit points when you inflicted some with a wielded
weapon.  It should have a very low percentage chance of creation and
certain vampires might wear them.  It would also only restore *lost*
hit points so a character couldn't increase their hit point total using
it when at maximum hit points.  It would also consume food like a regular
ring of regeneration (perhaps even slowly polymorph the wearer into an
undead creature).

